$9B21 ; the end mark
@res tables/checkpoint-rooms.bin

@module render ; The back buffer, the sprite plotter and the floor items
$9B22 c RenderPass ; Draw the five actors and the items of one main-loop pass and update the screen
> Called once a pass by the main loop at $9040 (26 517 calls over the 34 builds). The object slots from Hero $5B04 hold 5 records of 4 bytes (X, Y, frame, flags; slot 0 the hero).
> 1. For each slot BufferAddress $9CEE gives the back-buffer address of (X, Y) and the row plotter for X mod 8; both go to the slot's record in ActorRects $5B44 (slot + $40: the address, slot + $42: the plotter).
> 2. Each slot's sprite, frame F at $6000 + 72 * F, is drawn into the back buffer in three columns of 24 rows (DrawSpriteColumn $9C6A). Flags bit 7 set (facing right: the stored graphics face left) patches MirrorPatch1 and MirrorPatch2 $9C82/$9C87 to LD L,A / LD A,(HL), so each byte is bit-reversed through the table at $FE00, and draws the columns right to left; bit 7 clear patches them to two NOPs. The L1 arrivals record these writes in 21 builds ($9B82/$9B85 or $9B9C/$9B9F). After slot 0 WellFront $9A09 may paint the well over the hero.
> 3. DrawItems $9DE3 draws the floor items into the buffer and the screen at once. 4. CopyRects $9BEB copies the 4-byte by 24-row rectangles of last pass's positions (PrevActorRects $5B24, through IX = $5AE4) and of this pass's ($5B44) from the buffer to the screen: the old places get their background back and the new ones the sprites, with no flicker. 5. $5B44 is saved to $5B24, the alternate set's effects run ($92BD) and RestoreActorBackgrounds $9D3C repaints the buffer under the five actors from the tile map, ready for the next pass.
> Witness: L1 26515 (c1, explorers, gameover, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx).
$9B28 RenderPassAddresses ; step 1: 5 slots
$9B35 ; the plotter
$9B3B ; the back-buffer address
$9B52 RenderPassSprites ; step 2: 5 slots
$9B62 ; DE' = the sprite, $6000 + 72 * frame
$9B67 ; H' = the bit-reverse table's page $FE
$9B79 ; flags bit 7: mirrored (facing right)?
$9B7F ; bit 7 clear (facing left, as stored): no byte reversal, columns left to right
$9B99 RenderPassMirror ; bit 7 set (facing right): LD L,A / LD A,(HL) (bytes $6F, $7E), columns right to left
$9BB3 RenderPassNext
$9BB4 ; slot 0 (B = 5): the hero
$9BC4 ; step 3: the floor items
$9BC7 ; step 4: last pass's rectangles ($5B24 = $5AE4 + $40)
$9BCE ; this pass's rectangles
$9BD5 ; step 5: this pass's rectangles become last pass's
$9BE4 ; the alternate set's effects
$9BE7 ; repaint the buffer under the actors
$9BEB c CopyRects ; Copy five 4 x 24-byte rectangles from the back buffer to the screen
> IX = a 4-byte record array whose +$40/+$41 holds each rectangle's back-buffer address ($5B04 for ActorRects $5B44, $5AE4 for PrevActorRects $5B24). The screen address is the buffer address AND $5FFF. Each rectangle is 24 pixel rows of 4 bytes, copied with LDI and LDD on alternate rows (a serpentine, so the pointers never step back); NextCharRow $9DBF follows the character-row and third boundaries. B counts the rectangles; the entry CopyRect $9BF3 copies one (B = 1) from HL.
