@module sound ; The beeper sound effects
$C4B2 c PlaySound ; Play beeper sound A (0-6), HL, DE, BC kept
> Calls SoundDispatch $C4BC. The callers and their sounds: PickUpItem $BBF2 (2), DropFall $C051 (5), $A3E1 ($A484, 6), CreatureDeath $A89E ($A8A3, 3 every pass), DeathFrames $A8AB ($A8B7, the sound in C at the end of a death's frames: 0 for FallDeath $A888, 4 for Room1ADeath $A88F), $B0B6, $AA3C. The players are busy loops with the interrupts on: the L1 builds record 58 interrupts inside SoundChirp's loop ($C4D5-$C4D9, 16 builds), so the AY music of the IM 2 handler goes on and stretches the beeps: the half-wave the interrupt falls in grows by about 3700 T (the chirp's 7914 T to 11 679 T in c1 frame 23 509), and in the paper area the ULA's contention adds about 23 % (`EX (SP),HL` on the stack in RAM 5, `OUT ($FE)`); doc 9, 9.11.
> Witness: L1 339 (c1, explorers, gameover, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx).
$C4BC c SoundDispatch ; Jump to the player of sound A through SoundEffects $C55B, E = $10 (the speaker bit)
> A of 7 and more: nothing. The player's address is pushed and RET jumps to it.
> Witness: L1 339 (c1, explorers, gameover, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx).
$C4CE c SoundChirp ; Sound 2, the pickup: four bursts of 10 half-waves, each shorter (H $7F, $1F, 7, 1)
> Half-wave length H loop turns of OUT ($FE) with two EX (SP),HL of delay; H / 4 after each burst. Played by PickUpItem $BBF2 (and 1 more, 31 runs in 16 builds).
> Witness: L1 31 (c1, explorers, gameover, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx).
$C4D0 SoundChirpBurst
$C4D3 SoundChirpWave
$C4D5 SoundChirpDelay
$C4E9 c SoundRomFall ; Sound 1: 2250 bytes of ROM from $0000 to the speaker at a falling pitch, then sound 0
> HL = $0000, BC = 9000 counted down by 4: each byte of the ROM (AND E: the speaker bit) is sent after a delay of $23 - B loop turns, so the pitch falls as B does; then SoundRise $C506. Witness: gameover and rzx16000 play it once each.
> Witness: L1 2 (gameover, rzx).
$C4EF SoundRomFallLoop
$C4F6 SoundRomFallDelay
$C4F9 SoundRomFallByte
$C506 c SoundRise ; Sound 0: six bursts of 10 half-waves, each longer
> As SoundChirp $C4CE, H shifted up with a 1 bit after each burst: 6 bursts, H 3, 7, $0F, $1F, $3F, $7F. Played alone and at the end of SoundRomFall $C4E9. Witness: gameover (twice), rzx10000, rzx16000, rzx24000.
> Witness: L1 5 (gameover, rzx, rzx, rzx).
$C508 SoundRiseBurst
$C50B SoundRiseWave
$C50D SoundRiseDelay
$C521 c SoundHiss ; Sound 3: 100 bits from the ROM bytes $0000-$003F picked by R
> Witness: rzx06000, rzx22000, rzx32000 play it 11 times each (the status $18 handler CreatureDeath $A89E).
> Witness: L1 33 (rzx, rzx, rzx).
$C523 SoundHissLoop
$C532 c SoundBeepLow ; Sound 6: 3 square waves of $C8 loop turns a half
> HL = $03C8 (H the waves, L the half-wave), then SoundTone $C53A. Played by $A3E1 at $A484 (164 runs in 24 builds).
> Witness: L1 164 (gameover, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx).
$C537 c SoundBeepHigh ; Sound 5, the drop's splash: 5 square waves of $64 loop turns a half
> HL = $0564, then SoundTone $C53A (fallen into). Played by DropFall $C051, 105 times.
> Witness: L1 105 (rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx).
$C53A c SoundTone ; H square waves of 2 x L loop turns on the speaker (E the speaker bit)
> Witness: L1 1017 (gameover, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx).
$C53E SoundToneLow
$C544 SoundToneHigh
$C54A c SoundRomBurst ; Sound 4: 1280 bytes of ROM from $0000 to the speaker, no delay
> HL = $0000 (LD HL,$0000: SK took its operand for NOPs), B = 0: 256 x 5 bytes, each AND E sent. Witness: rzx10000 plays it once.
> Witness: L1 1 (rzx).
$C54E SoundRomBurstLoop
$C550 SoundRomBurstByte
$C55B w SoundEffects 1 ; The players of the sounds 0-6
> Read by SoundDispatch $C4BC: 0 SoundRise, 1 SoundRomFall, 2 SoundChirp, 3 SoundHiss, 4 SoundRomBurst, 5 SoundBeepHigh, 6 SoundBeepLow. Witness: the play builds read the words of the sounds they play.
$C569 b SoundPad ; Leftover: 23 bytes ($00 x 16, then $03 $00 $02 $00 $01 $00 $00) no code refers to
> No instruction of the image names an address in $C569-$C57F and no play build reads them (the load in c1 only); known only from the code.

@module tiles ; The tiles: pixels, attributes, the alternate upper set
$C580 b AltTileAttrs 16 ; The attributes of the alternate upper tiles $80-$FF
> In a room of AltTilesetRooms $9AC6 the room builder swaps these 128 bytes with TileAttrs+$80 ($DF80) and AltTilePixels $FA00 with TilePixels+$400 ($F400); the next room on the base set swaps them back. Witness: written and read in the rzx builds 18000, 20000, 26000 (the swap $918D).
@res tiles/alt-attrs.bin

@module rooms
$C600 b BackTiles 16 ; The 256 back-tiles: 4 x 4 tile indices each
> A room is 8 x 4 back-tiles (RoomGrids $D600), each 4 x 4 tiles of 8 x 8 pixels: the 32 x 16 tile map TileMap $F800. 242 of the 256 back-tiles appear in the 72 grids; the highest index used is $F4.
> Witness: the play builds read the back-tiles of the rooms they enter (bytemap.py).
@res rooms/backtiles.bin
$D600 b RoomGrids 8 ; The back-tile grids of the 72 rooms: 32 bytes a room, 4 rows of 8
> Room R (level x 12 + column) at $D600 + 32 x R. Rooms $46 and $47, past the 70 of the map, exist in both tables and are portal targets; their records in RoomCreatures $C152 are zeros.
> Witness: each room the recordings enter has its 32 bytes read (bytemap.py; 57 rooms in the RZX run).
@res rooms/grids.bin

@module tiles
$DF00 b TileAttrs 16 ; The attribute of each of the 256 tiles
> Looked up by tile index (A + $DF00) when the tile map is drawn ($99A4). $DF80-$DFFF are swapped with AltTileAttrs $C580 in the rooms on the alternate set.
@res tiles/base-attrs.bin

@module render
$E000 b BackBuffer 32 ; The back buffer of the play window in play; on the tape and between games the saved panel's pixels
> In play RenderPass $9B22 composes the actors here, at the screen address + $A000 (rows of the play window in the screen's layout, $E000-$EFFF for the top two thirds), copies their rectangles to the screen and repaints the background from the tile map. Before play the first 2048 bytes are the status panel (the screen's bottom third, $5000-$57FF): InitGame copies them to the screen at $8F8B, DeviceMenu $93AF cuts the menu's frame from them, and EndGame $906D saves the panel back here when a game ends.
> Witness: c1 reads all of $E000-$E8FF (byte counts, page 0; InitGame copies them before the first game, and EndGame has not run yet); every play build reads and writes $DF80-$EFFF.
@res screens/panel-pixels.bin
$E800 b SavedPanelAttrs 32 ; The saved panel's attributes (8 rows of 32), part of the back buffer in play
> Copied to $5A00 by InitGame ($8F8B); saved again by EndGame $906D ($907B).
@res screens/panel-attrs.bin
$E900 b BackBufferLeftover 16 ; The rest of the back buffer: on the tape, leftover bytes play overwrites
> No code reads these 1792 bytes before play writes them. They hold, among other bytes, twelve 24 x 24 sprites in the sprite layout at $E930-$EC8F that no frame number reaches (the sprite space ends at $A7B8; SK names three of them "captured by the snake"), drawn in src/res/leftovers/README.md.
@res leftovers/back-buffer-tape.bin

@module tiles
$F000 b TilePixels 8 ; The 256 tiles' pixels: 8 bytes each
> Tile T at $F000 + 8 x T (the word $5B72 = $F000). $F400-$F7FF (tiles $80-$FF) are swapped with AltTilePixels $FA00 in the rooms on the alternate set.
@res tiles/base-pixels.bin

@module rooms
$F800 b TileMap 32 ; The room's tile map, 32 x 16 tile indices, rebuilt on each room entry; on the tape a leftover
> The builder ($9914) writes it from the room's grid and the back-tiles before anything reads it. The 512 bytes on the tape are the map of a room as it was when the tape was saved.
@res leftovers/tile-map-tape.bin

@module tiles
$FA00 b AltTilePixels 8 ; The alternate upper tiles $80-$FF: 128 tiles of 8 bytes
@res tiles/alt-pixels.bin

@module render
$FE00 b BitReverse 16 ; Byte n reversed bit for bit: mirrors a sprite byte for the mirrored (right-facing) frames
> Checked: all 256 bytes equal n reversed. Read by the mirror patch of the plotter ($9C82/$9C87).
$FF00 b OutlineMasks 16 ; The outline mask of byte n: NOT (n OR n << 1 OR n >> 1)
> Checked: all 256 bytes follow the formula. RenderPass ANDs the screen with the mask of the rows above, at and below a sprite row, so a sprite gets a one-pixel black outline.
